## Piece 2 — Per-CPU Storage

### Goal

Give every CPU (BSP included) a private struct reachable via `GS` base, so
that current-thread pointer, lock-tracking stack, syscall scratch, and
`TSS.RSP0`/IST stacks stop being global statics and become per-CPU state.
This is the prerequisite for letting more than one CPU safely run kernel
code at all.

### What it needs to do

- Define a `per_cpu_t` struct: CPU index, APIC ID, current-thread pointer,
  the existing bounded lock-tracking stack from `spinlock.c` (moved in, not
  reimplemented), syscall entry scratch (the kernel RSP staging used around
  `SWAPGS` in `syscall_entry.asm`), and a pointer to this CPU's own TSS.
- Allocate one `per_cpu_t` per usable CPU discovered in Piece 1 (array sized
  from the bounded count established there).
- Every CPU — BSP included, at the point it's re-architected, and each AP
  right after being released — sets `GS_BASE` and `KERNEL_GS_BASE` (via WRMSR)
  to point at its own `per_cpu_t` before doing anything that reads
  current-thread state or touches the lock tracker.
- `gdt.c` changes from one shared TSS to one TSS per CPU, each with its own
  `RSP0`, IST1 (#DF), and IST2 (NMI) stacks. These cannot be shared — two
  CPUs faulting into the same #DF/NMI stack simultaneously would corrupt
  each other's frames.
- Migrate the existing global "current thread" and lock-tracking-stack
  statics to be per-CPU-struct members, reached through GS-relative
  addressing, not renamed globals.

### Binding invariants

| ID | Invariant | How to check |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SM6 | Every access to current-thread, the lock-tracking stack, or syscall scratch goes through the per-CPU struct via GS base. No remaining global statics for these three. | Grep for the old global symbol names; confirm none remain outside the per-CPU struct definition itself. |
| SM7 | Each CPU has its own `RSP0`, IST1 (#DF), and IST2 (NMI) stacks. No two CPUs ever share a fault or NMI stack. | Inspect per-CPU TSS construction in `gdt.c`; confirm stack pointers are distinct allocations indexed by CPU. |
| SM8 | GS base (and `KERNEL_GS_BASE`) must be set before a CPU executes any code that reads current-thread state or acquires a lock — including immediately at AP release from Piece 1. | Trace the release path; confirm the WRMSR happens before the first lock acquisition or scheduler touch. |
| SM9 | NMI entry (IST2) must not assume a particular `SWAPGS` state — an NMI can land mid-transition on the user/kernel boundary on any CPU. Existing exact-boundary NMI testing (5 syscall transitions × BSP) must be re-derived per-CPU, not assumed to generalize. | Extend `test-nmi`-style boundary injection to run against an AP, not just the BSP. |
| SM10 | The per-CPU struct, once allocated at bring-up, is never freed or reallocated. Matches Piece 1's "no hot-plug" scope. | Confirm no free/realloc path exists for `per_cpu_t` entries. |

### Verification plan (not yet run)

- Host: unit test for `per_cpu_t` layout/alignment and GS-relative offset
  macros, independent of real hardware.
- QEMU: `-smp N`, confirm each AP's lock-tracking stack starts empty and
  independent; confirm BSP behavior is byte-for-byte unchanged at `-smp 1`.
- Extend `make test-nmi` to inject at an AP, not only the BSP, once APs can
  safely take interrupts (this piece enables that; scheduling them is Piece 4).

### What this piece does NOT do

- Does not dispatch scheduler work to APs. An AP with per-CPU storage set up
  can safely run a trivial idle loop, but does not join the run/wait queues.
- Does not verify lock rank checking under real cross-CPU contention — moving
  the tracking stack per-CPU is necessary but not sufficient; that
  verification is Piece 3.
- Does not change user-process CR3 switching semantics.

