## Piece 3 — Lock Discipline Under Real Concurrency

### Goal

Confirm the rank-checking and mutual-exclusion machinery in `spinlock.c`
is actually correct when multiple CPUs genuinely contend on the same lock at
the same time, not merely interleaved via preemption on one core.

### What it needs to do

- Audit the lock body itself: acquisition must use a real atomic
  read-modify-write (a `LOCK`-prefixed exchange or compare-exchange) that is
  correct under cache coherency across cores. IRQ masking alone only
  protects against local reentrancy on the same core — it does nothing
  against a second core spinning on the same lock. Confirm the existing
  implementation already does this (x86 spinlocks normally do) rather than
  assuming it.
- Keep rank tracking per-CPU (from Piece 2): each CPU's own held-lock stack.
  Local rank-order violations (this CPU acquiring out of order) are still
  caught the same way as today.
- Explicitly document what this checker does *not* catch: a genuine
  cross-CPU deadlock where CPU A holds rank 2 while waiting on rank 1 held by
  CPU B is a liveness/ordering problem between two independent per-CPU
  stacks, not a single-stack local violation. Don't let the existing panic
  message imply broader coverage than this.
- Re-verify the scheduler/ext2 rank-1 mutual exclusivity rule under actual
  concurrent scheduler activity from more than one core, not single-CPU
  interleaving.

### Binding invariants

| ID | Invariant | How to check |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SM11 | Lock acquisition uses a real atomic exchange visible across cores; IRQ masking is necessary but not sufficient and must not be relied on alone for cross-CPU exclusion. | Read the lock body in `spinlock.c`; confirm a `lock`-prefixed instruction (or equivalent intrinsic) guards the critical section, independent of IRQ state. |
| SM12 | Rank checking remains a per-CPU, local-only invariant. It is documented as unable to detect cross-CPU circular waits, rather than silently implying full deadlock coverage. | Check the panic/documentation text describes the actual scope; confirm no claim of cross-CPU inversion detection exists without the mechanism to back it. |
| SM13 | Rank 1 mutual exclusivity between scheduler and ext2 locks holds under genuine concurrent access from independent CPUs, not just serialized preemption. | Stress test: one CPU driving scheduler activity, another driving ext2 I/O, concurrently; confirm no rank violation and no corruption. |

### Verification plan (not yet run)

- Host: if the host test harness can simulate concurrent lock operations
  (multiple simulated "CPUs" hammering a shared lock structure), add that
  matrix here; otherwise this piece is QEMU/hardware-only for the
  concurrency claim itself.
- QEMU: multi-core boot with deliberate contention — multiple APs hammering
  a shared structure (console lock, heap lock) simultaneously; confirm no
  double-acquire, no corruption, and existing single-CPU test suites still
  pass unchanged at `-smp 1`.

### What this piece does NOT do

- Does not add lock-free data structures.
- Does not solve cross-CPU deadlock detection — documents the limitation
  instead (SM12).
- Does not yet let a full scheduler run across CPUs — that's Piece 4; this
  piece only proves the locking primitives themselves are safe once more
  than one CPU can reach them.

