## Piece 4 — Scheduler

### Goal

Let APs actually run threads. Start with a single global runqueue and a
single scheduler lock (rank 1, still mutually exclusive with ext2 per the
existing rule) rather than per-CPU runqueues, to stay close to the current
single-CPU model while proving real parallel execution works.

### What it needs to do

- The existing run/wait queues in `thread.c` become genuinely
  multi-CPU-accessed structures, protected by the scheduler lock — not
  reimplemented, just correctly shared.
- Each CPU gets its own idle thread to run when the shared runqueue is empty
  for that CPU.
- `switch_context` becomes per-CPU-aware: it reads/writes the current-thread
  pointer and updates `TSS.RSP0` (both from Piece 2's per-CPU struct) for the
  CPU actually executing the switch — never another CPU's.
- Each CPU's own LAPIC timer (already initialized for the BSP per existing
  `apic.c` code) must be initialized for each AP too, and drives preemption
  independently per core, contending on the shared scheduler lock exactly
  like any other rank-1 access.
- Waking a thread that should run on an idle CPU other than the one doing
  the waking needs a cross-CPU "kick." Until Piece 5's IPI mechanism lands,
  this can be best-effort (the target CPU notices on its next timer tick) —
  document this explicitly as a latency bound, not a promise.

### Binding invariants

| ID | Invariant | How to check |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SM14 | The scheduler lock is the single source of truth for all run/wait queue mutation across every CPU in this piece. No per-CPU runqueue exists yet. | Confirm every run/wait queue mutation site acquires the scheduler lock, regardless of which CPU calls it. |
| SM15 | `switch_context` updates only the executing CPU's own `TSS.RSP0` and current-thread pointer, never another CPU's per-CPU struct. | Trace `switch_context` call sites under multi-core; confirm no cross-CPU struct writes. |
| SM16 | Cross-CPU wake before Piece 5 lands is best-effort (poll-based, bounded by timer tick period) and documented as such — no real-time wake latency is claimed. | Check wake-path comments/docs match the actual mechanism; revisit once IPI-based kick exists. |
| SM17 | L2 (no spinlock held across `switch_context`) holds identically per-CPU under concurrent switches on multiple cores, not just single-CPU preemption. | Stress test concurrent switches across CPUs; assert via `spin_debug_assert_unheld` on every core, not just the BSP. |

### Verification plan (not yet run)

- QEMU: spawn N processes where N exceeds the core count; confirm all
  complete, confirm exit status and resource reclamation are correct.
- Extend the existing PMM/heap allocation-set audit (`pmm_snapshot`,
  `vmm_kernel_mapping_fingerprint`) to a quiescent point after concurrent
  frees from different CPUs — confirm bitmaps and fingerprints still
  balance exactly as they do today for the single-CPU case.

### What this piece does NOT do

- No per-CPU runqueues or load balancing — explicitly deferred.
- No CPU affinity API.
- No real-time scheduling guarantees.
- Does not yet implement IPI-based wake — that's Piece 5; this piece uses
  the best-effort polling fallback described in SM16.

