## Piece 6 — Re-audit of the Two-Stage PMM/VMM Init

### Goal

Confirm the existing single-threaded design — PMM capped at 1 GiB until
`vmm_init` builds the full HHDM and switches CR3, then
`pmm_unlock_high_memory()` — still holds correctly once APs exist. This
touches a listed do-not-touch-without-discussion item (AGENTS.md §9); this
piece *is* that discussion.

### What it needs to do

- Adopt the simplest safe answer, stated explicitly rather than left open:
  AP release (the end of Piece 1/2's bring-up) is gated strictly behind the
  BSP completing `pmm_unlock_high_memory()`. APs do not begin bring-up
  concurrently with BSP VMM construction; bring-up is sequenced after it.
  This preserves the existing invariant without redesigning it, consistent
  with AGENTS.md §9's framing of preserving behavior over preserving
  specific lines of code.
- Confirm all CPUs share the single kernel-owned PML4 (the existing "shared
  kernel PML4 ownership" do-not-touch item is unaffected) — this piece adds
  per-CPU stacks and TSS state within that shared mapping, not per-CPU
  address spaces for kernel context.
- Re-confirm that no AP allocation path can reach above Limine's HHDM
  coverage before the kernel's own full HHDM (built once, by the BSP) is
  active globally.

### Binding invariants

| ID | Invariant | How to check |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SM21 | AP release must not occur before `pmm_unlock_high_memory()` has completed on the BSP. | Boot-log ordering assertion: confirm no AP alive-flag/release event appears before the `[PMM] High-memory allocation unlocked` log line. |
| SM22 | Once released, APs do not construct or switch to a separate PML4 for kernel context. All CPUs share the single kernel-owned PML4; this piece adds only per-CPU stacks/TSS state within it. | Confirm AP bring-up loads the existing shared kernel CR3 value, not a newly built one. |

### Verification plan (not yet run)

- QEMU: `-smp N` boot log inspection confirming the ordering in SM21 holds
  on every boot, not just observed once.
- Physical: Dell 5590/5530 boot log capture (same style as the existing
  32 GiB RAM evidence in ROADMAP.md §9H) confirming the same ordering on
  real firmware.

### What this piece does NOT do

- Does not implement per-process/per-CPU distinct CR3 outside of existing
  user-process switching — that's already handled by `switch_context` and is
  unrelated to this piece.
- Does not revisit user address-space CR3 switching semantics.
- Does not raise `PMM_BITMAP_CAPACITY_BYTES` or otherwise change the RAM
  coverage established in Phase 9H — orthogonal concern.
